{
  "dom_id": "step-q341450",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ | ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nIf $h(x)=(-2f(x)-3)(g(x)+1),$ find the value of ${h}^{\u2032}(2).$\n\n${h}^{\u2032}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Explanation:\n\nThe product rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(uv)=u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-2f(x)-3$ and $v=g(x)+1.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{f}^{\u2032}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(x) & =-2{f}^{\u2032}(x)(g(x)+1)+(-2f(x)-3){g}^{\u2032}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{\u2032}(2) & =-2{f}^{\u2032}(2)(g(2)+1)+(-2f(2)-3){g}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\  & =-2(2)(-3+1)+(-2(4)-3)(5) \\\\  & =-4(-2)+(-8-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =8+(-11)(5) \\\\  & =8-55 \\\\  & =-47.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
